Why? Well, for strategic reasons.

52
00:22:04,760 --> 00:22:08,020
So, suppose that I'm offering you a job

53
00:22:08,020 --> 00:22:09,780
as a system professor of economics

54
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:11,520
and I say how does $200,000 sound?

55
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:13,040
All right.

56
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:14,660
Now, one thing you might say, you know,

57
00:22:14,660 --> 00:22:15,780
$2,000, $100,000 a year.

58
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:18,000
You might just say sold, awesome.

59
00:22:18,420 --> 00:22:19,540
On the other hand, you might say,

60
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:21,360
man this guy is really dumb.

61
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:23,900
He doesn't have any idea what the market is like.

62
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:28,220
So, you might say $200,000, how would I survive on that

63
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:30,020
and start complaining about it.

64
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:33,500
I mean, I hear that like everybody gets that at least.

65
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:35,360
I mean, you could just start complaining

66
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:38,480
about my offer despite the fact that it's a great deal for you.

67
00:22:38,760 --> 00:22:40,000
Now, do not try this at home.

68
00:22:40,280 --> 00:22:44,540
But, I'm sure you have noticed times when you propose a deal

69
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:45,880
that is really good for somebody

70
00:22:45,880 --> 00:22:47,300
and they start complaining about it.

71
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:49,320
So, maybe they're just confused

72
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:51,480
but another possibility is they're very clever

73
00:22:51,480 --> 00:22:54,080
and they sense in your generous offer weakness.

74
00:22:54,740 --> 00:22:56,860
And, from that weakness they hope to profit

75
00:22:56,860 --> 00:22:58,020
by asking for even more.

76
00:22:58,020 --> 00:23:00,920
This is really blatant if you try offering kids a deal

77
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:03,120
and say, hey kids, how would you like to have three chocolates?

78
00:23:03,120 --> 00:23:05,660
So, I'm only three, how can you give me three?

79
00:23:05,660 --> 00:23:06,480
And, they start crying.

80
00:23:06,480 --> 00:23:08,820
You're like, man, before I said anything you had no chocolates.

81
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:12,100
I just offered you three and now you're crying at me.

82
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:14,560
So, but they're trying to get more.

83
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:16,460
I'm going to give you the chocolate.

84
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:17,140
All right.

85
00:23:17,140 --> 00:23:21,580
So, and sometimes with certain kinds of weak parents this works.

86
00:23:21,580 --> 00:23:22,580
And, they start crying.

87
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:24,740
You offer them a favor and then they complain

88
00:23:24,740 --> 00:23:25,820
and then they get even more.

89
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:26,900
All right.

90
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:31,340
So, what this means is that there's a big difference

91
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:34,500
between a standard of a Pareto improvement

92
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:37,020
and what could be called a demonstrative preference

93
00:23:37,020 --> 00:23:40,220
standard where everyone says, yes, I agree, this is fantastic.

94
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:41,740
All right.

95
00:23:41,740 --> 00:23:44,080
So, if you are fond of Austrian economics,

96
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:45,040
sometimes they will talk

97
00:23:45,040 --> 00:23:47,320
about the demonstrative preference standard.

98
00:23:47,640 --> 00:23:49,300
The Pareto standard is different from that

99
00:23:49,300 --> 00:23:51,020
because it admits the possibility

100
00:23:51,460 --> 00:23:54,100
that you could deliver a Pareto improvement

101
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:57,100
that is universally denounced and everyone says, no way,

102
00:23:57,100 --> 00:23:59,700
I hate your idea and I hate you.

103
00:24:00,020 --> 00:24:02,580
And, I absolutely refuse to go along with it.

104
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:05,540
And, then you do it and everyone may be quietly thinking, wow,

105
00:24:05,540 --> 00:24:07,000
this is way better than what I had before.

106
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:08,020
All right.

107
00:24:08,020 --> 00:24:08,760
So, that's possible.

108
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:11,120
Does this mean that it's always that way?

109
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:11,840
No, of course not.

110
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:15,800
But, you know, the Pareto improvement standard is not

111
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:18,900
in any way the same as everyone vocally agrees

112
00:24:18,900 --> 00:24:19,940
that they like what you're offering.

113
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:22,500
And, it's possible in theory.

114
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:25,620
And, you have probably observed plenty of times in practice

115
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,260
where someone has actually bought an idea

116
00:24:29,260 --> 00:24:30,360
that would be an improvement for them.

117
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:31,520
Why do they do that?

118
00:24:31,880 --> 00:24:32,900
Because they want to, because they,

119
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:34,540
is it because they don't like improvements?

120
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:35,920
No, it's because they want more.

121
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:38,720
And, if you offer them something, then they sense

122
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:40,260
that maybe there's even more where that came from.

123
00:24:40,540 --> 00:24:40,940
Yes.

124
00:24:40,940 --> 00:24:43,980
I, from what I understand from demonstrated preference is

125
00:24:43,980 --> 00:24:46,460
that it's the thing that people actually, in fact,

126
00:24:46,460 --> 00:24:49,060
would choose if given the option, not what they say.

127
00:24:49,800 --> 00:24:52,220
So, someone who says I would buy magazines

128
00:24:52,220 --> 00:24:55,180
with normal looking women in them, but when they actually go

129
00:24:55,180 --> 00:24:56,760
and buy magazines, they buy ones with.

130
00:24:56,760 --> 00:25:01,460
So, that, so, like, the answer again is there is a multiplicity

131
00:25:01,460 --> 00:25:02,740
of the language that people use.

132
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:04,740
But, among Austrian economists

133
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:06,420
who like the demonstrated preference standard,

134
00:25:06,780 --> 00:25:09,860
really the goal, really the only way you can actually truly

135
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:11,980
say that something does, does improve people

136
00:25:11,980 --> 00:25:13,120
in this demonstrated preference standard is

137
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:14,180
if they in fact make the trade.

138
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:18,380
So, if you go and pass the hat for,

139
00:25:18,380 --> 00:25:20,780
to raise $100,000 for asteroid deflection

140
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:23,720
and everyone, otherwise everyone dies and you don't manage

141
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:26,500
to raise any money, this would actually by the standard show

142
00:25:26,500 --> 00:25:29,020
that people do not in fact prefer the donation

143
00:25:29,020 --> 00:25:31,820
to getting destroyed, even though that may seem strange.

144
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:33,500
But, yes.

145
00:25:33,500 --> 00:25:35,300
So, and again, sometimes, well,

146
00:25:35,300 --> 00:25:36,900
it's just that they haven't proven that they,

147
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:38,920
that they actually would rather donate

148
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:40,420
or that they prefer the money to,

149
00:25:40,420 --> 00:25:42,660
or they prefer not being destroyed to donating money.

150
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:42,980
But, yes.

151
00:25:42,980 --> 00:25:46,260
There is this other point of there's a difference

152
00:25:46,260 --> 00:25:47,300
between what people say

153
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:48,900
and what actually makes them better off.

154
00:25:48,900 --> 00:25:52,700
And, when we get to psychology and economics, we'll talk a bit

155
00:25:52,700 --> 00:25:56,260
about what psychologists call social desirability bias,

156
00:25:56,260 --> 00:25:58,300
tendency of people to say what sounds good rather

157
00:25:58,300 --> 00:26:01,900
than what's true, which is a very interesting field

158
00:26:01,900 --> 00:26:03,900
of research because it is the empirical proof

159
00:26:03,900 --> 00:26:04,940
of what economists have been saying

160
00:26:04,940 --> 00:26:06,340
without any evidence for all this time.

161
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:10,780
Namely, that people often actually say one thing

162
00:26:10,780 --> 00:26:12,020
but want another thing.

163
00:26:12,020 --> 00:26:14,220
I can see most of the economists I talk to, it's just like,

164
00:26:14,220 --> 00:26:15,620
well, people could be lying.

165
00:26:15,620 --> 00:26:17,420
I suppose they could be, but do you have any actual evidence

166
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:18,220
for that?

167
00:26:18,220 --> 00:26:19,420
Prove that they're not lying.

168
00:26:19,420 --> 00:26:20,820
Sounds kind of paranoid.

169
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:24,300
But, psychologists actually do try

170
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:27,060
to document real empirical differences

171
00:26:27,060 --> 00:26:31,100
between what people say and what people in fact do when they are

172
00:26:31,100 --> 00:26:33,540
in that situation, such as which magazines they're going

173
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:35,500
to buy, which magazines they're going to watch.

174
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:38,140
Which magazines they're going to buy.

175
00:26:38,140 --> 00:26:39,140
All right.

176
00:26:39,140 --> 00:26:42,780
Now, in a highly stylized theoretical setting, right,

177
00:26:42,780 --> 00:26:45,340
and what is a highly stylized theoretical setting?

178
00:26:45,340 --> 00:26:49,080
It's a homework problem or a model in a journal.

179
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:50,380
All right.

180
00:26:50,380 --> 00:26:52,380
That's what we mean by a highly stylized theoretical setting.

181
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:54,400
It's one where we construct it perfectly

182
00:26:54,400 --> 00:26:57,600
so that all the pieces fit nicely, right.

183
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:01,140
In these settings, Pareto improvements are conceivable.

184
00:27:01,140 --> 00:27:02,440
So, in settings like this,

185
00:27:02,440 --> 00:27:04,260
Pareto improvements are conceivable.

186
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:06,460
For example, if we have a world

187
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:09,180
where everyone has identical preferences and endowments

188
00:27:09,180 --> 00:27:11,640
and there's an externality.

189
00:27:11,640 --> 00:27:11,840
All right.

190
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:14,180
So, how do we get that?

191
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:15,940
Well, you know, you just think about homework problems,

192
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:16,220
you're done.

193
00:27:16,220 --> 00:27:17,940
Everyone has identical preferences.

194
00:27:17,940 --> 00:27:19,980
Everyone has identical endowments.

195
00:27:19,980 --> 00:27:23,020
There's a negative externality of pollution.

196
00:27:23,020 --> 00:27:24,320
Everyone's polluting.

197
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:27,340
And what would be a, what could be a Pareto improvement

198
00:27:27,340 --> 00:27:29,300
if we just point a gun at everybody's head and say,

199
00:27:29,300 --> 00:27:33,340
hey everybody, pollute 5% less now.

200
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:34,740
All right. And in this world,

201
00:27:34,740 --> 00:27:38,060
it is possible that's a Pareto improvement.

202
00:27:38,060 --> 00:27:43,580
Like so. And that's the whole set up of a model like that.

203
00:27:43,580 --> 00:27:44,820
So, that is possible.

204
00:27:44,820 --> 00:27:46,660
And you might say that people don't like being coerced.

205
00:27:46,660 --> 00:27:47,740
Well, they don't like being coerced,

206
00:27:47,740 --> 00:27:50,060
but they also do like sometimes.

207
00:27:50,060 --> 00:27:53,100
And in fact, in this model, they definitely like what happens

208
00:27:53,100 --> 00:27:55,420
when other people besides themselves are coerced.

209
00:27:55,420 --> 00:27:59,660
And we can set the model up very easily so that the dislike you

210
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:02,180
experience of having the gun at your head is much,

211
00:28:02,180 --> 00:28:05,220
is smaller than the like that you get for the benefits

212
00:28:05,220 --> 00:28:07,820
of the gun being pointed at the heads of everybody but yourself

213
00:28:07,820 --> 00:28:10,380
for a net positive of the gun being pointed

214
00:28:10,380 --> 00:28:11,980
at everybody's head.

215
00:28:11,980 --> 00:28:12,140
All right.

216
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:16,180
So, that is totally doable in a model.

217
00:28:16,180 --> 00:28:17,460
All right.

218
00:28:17,460 --> 00:28:21,660
Okay. But, but, there's a very strong argument

219
00:28:21,660 --> 00:28:23,500
in the real world, right.

220
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:26,060
The real world is different from a homework problem.

221
00:28:26,060 --> 00:28:27,660
The real world is different from a model

222
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:29,420
that you publish in a journal.

223
00:28:29,420 --> 00:28:32,020
The homework problem or the model, you can tailor it

224
00:28:32,020 --> 00:28:36,460
to make it work to be just the way you want to prove your point.

225
00:28:36,460 --> 00:28:38,740
But, the real world, you don't get to do that.

226
00:28:38,740 --> 00:28:40,500
Right. The real world, you don't get

227
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:43,540
to actually make everything work just so to make it perfect.

228
00:28:43,540 --> 00:28:44,700
At least, usually you don't.

229
00:28:44,700 --> 00:28:46,180
I never have.

230
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:48,660
I've never been able to make the world perfect so far.

231
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:50,620
I don't know if anyone else had this opportunity.

232
00:28:50,620 --> 00:28:51,900
Like, this is my day.

233
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:54,740
I'm doing everything right.

234
00:28:54,740 --> 00:28:55,060
All right.

235
00:28:55,060 --> 00:28:57,820
So, in the real world, there's a strong argument

236
00:28:57,820 --> 00:29:00,460
that two weird things are true.

237
00:29:00,460 --> 00:29:01,180
All right.

238
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:05,820
So, weird thing number one, everything's Pareto efficient.

239
00:29:05,820 --> 00:29:06,620
All right.

240
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:13,140
So, that's weird thing number one.

241
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:16,380
And, weird thing number two is Pareto improvements

242
00:29:16,380 --> 00:29:18,060
are impossible.

243
00:29:18,060 --> 00:29:18,780
All right.

244
00:29:18,780 --> 00:29:24,700
Now, I should warn you.

245
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:27,300
These are two things that are almost never mentioned

246
00:29:27,300 --> 00:29:29,340
in this class, in a way when people teach this class

247
00:29:29,340 --> 00:29:30,740
around the world.

248
00:29:30,740 --> 00:29:33,260
And, yet, they are almost certainly true,

249
00:29:33,260 --> 00:29:37,300
as I think I can convince you if you think about the real world.

250
00:29:37,300 --> 00:29:37,540
All right.

251
00:29:37,540 --> 00:29:39,700
So, why would these things be true?

252
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:44,980
Simple. It's highly unlikely that any change will literally

253
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:46,340
hurt no one.

254
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:50,060
Any change that you can imagine is going to have some person

255
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:53,260
who loses at least a small amount.

256
00:29:53,260 --> 00:29:55,860
Furthermore, while many economists wave their hands

257
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:57,740
and say, well, we just compensate the losers.

258
00:29:57,740 --> 00:30:00,980
In the real world, compensating losers is really hard.

259
00:30:00,980 --> 00:30:04,180
And, remember, if it's to be a genuine Pareto improvement,

260
00:30:04,180 --> 00:30:05,980
what percent of the losers do we have

261
00:30:05,980 --> 00:30:07,900
to actually successfully compensate?

262
00:30:07,900 --> 00:30:09,580
One hundred percent.

263
00:30:09,580 --> 00:30:11,860
We can't miss a single loser.

264
00:30:11,860 --> 00:30:14,780
Furthermore, even if we compensate every loser,

265
00:30:14,780 --> 00:30:17,020
we have to make sure that we give everyone compensation,

266
00:30:17,020 --> 00:30:19,460
which is at least as good as whatever he lost.

267
00:30:19,460 --> 00:30:21,420
So, it's not enough just to give everybody a little bit

268
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:22,100
of compensation.

269
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:24,380
Say, well, everybody, all right, I found all the losers,

270
00:30:24,380 --> 00:30:26,060
and I gave them each five bucks of compensation.

271
00:30:26,060 --> 00:30:27,260
Problem solved.

272
00:30:27,260 --> 00:30:28,620
Well, if there's one person who says, yeah,

273
00:30:28,620 --> 00:30:31,380
but I lost by five dollars and one cent.

274
00:30:31,380 --> 00:30:34,260
So, you still have failed to create a Pareto improvement.

275
00:30:34,260 --> 00:30:35,420
Of course, he might be saying that,

276
00:30:35,420 --> 00:30:38,340
even though he gained from the change.

277
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:40,860
He might just be going and being a squeaky wheel,

278
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:42,540
hoping to get some grease.

279
00:30:42,540 --> 00:30:46,180
But, in the real world, what are the odds

280
00:30:46,180 --> 00:30:48,420
that there's nobody genuinely like that?

281
00:30:48,420 --> 00:30:51,180
Nobody who actually lost more than the other people,

282
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:53,020
and you have failed to adequately compensate,

283
00:30:53,020 --> 00:30:55,940
you've missed no one, that is really pretty fanciful.

284
00:30:55,940 --> 00:30:58,820
So, think about one of the most mundane transactions

285
00:30:58,820 --> 00:30:59,900
in the world.

286
00:30:59,900 --> 00:31:02,180
Let's see, does anyone even own a watch anymore?

287
00:31:02,180 --> 00:31:06,980
They were more common when I first wrote these notes.

288
00:31:06,980 --> 00:31:11,220
All right, so, suppose I want to buy, what is this?

289
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:12,700
What is that?

290
00:31:12,700 --> 00:31:15,140
Suppose I want to buy whatever that is.

291
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:20,220
Actually, it is better to be a watch.

292
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:25,460
So, suppose that I go and buy somebody's watch.

293
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:28,740
All right, now, the very easy thing is,

294
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:29,620
it's very easy to say,

295
00:31:29,620 --> 00:31:31,020
well, that was clearly a Pareto improvement

296
00:31:31,020 --> 00:31:33,060
because you accepted my offer

297
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:35,060
because you value the money more than the watch.

298
00:31:35,060 --> 00:31:37,140
I made the offer because I value the watch

299
00:31:37,140 --> 00:31:39,340
more than the money, problem solved.

300
00:31:39,340 --> 00:31:42,140
But, there are other people affected.

301
00:31:42,140 --> 00:31:45,260
For example, anyone who ever would have asked you the time

302
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:47,260
is now going to be affected.

303
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:48,900
Right, so, anyone who might have asked you the time

304
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:50,820
is now going to be slightly inconvenienced

305
00:31:50,820 --> 00:31:54,140
because, rather than being able to see you off the bat

306
00:31:54,140 --> 00:31:55,620
and ask you what the time is,

307
00:31:55,620 --> 00:31:57,300
they're going to have to search a little bit harder.

308
00:31:57,300 --> 00:31:59,940
So, the loser here is in absolute sense,

309
00:31:59,940 --> 00:32:00,860
now you value the watch.

310
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:01,860
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

311
00:32:01,860 --> 00:32:03,780
They are just, they're a little bit worse off

312
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:05,980
than they were before because now they have,

313
00:32:05,980 --> 00:32:07,820
they are slightly inconvenienced.

314
00:32:07,820 --> 00:32:08,660
Now, of course, you can say,

315
