182
01:29:13,640 --> 01:29:18,720
Manuel Kant, who has thousands of pages of mostly gibberish that people have been arguing

183
01:29:18,720 --> 01:29:21,440
about the meaning of for 200 years.

184
01:29:21,440 --> 01:29:24,480
Because, you know, there is a whole journal called Kantstudien.

185
01:29:24,480 --> 01:29:29,400
If you look at it, in 1851, what did Germans think Edward Kant really meant?

186
01:29:29,400 --> 01:29:30,600
How about 1857?

187
01:29:30,600 --> 01:29:31,600
How about 1888?

188
01:29:31,600 --> 01:29:33,080
It just goes on and on.

189
01:29:33,080 --> 01:29:36,600
Anyway, nothing like that for Epicurus because he was clear, said it right the first time.

190
01:29:36,600 --> 01:29:41,440
Anyway, so Epicurus has a number of pieces of advice for people who want to be happier.

191
01:29:41,440 --> 01:29:46,120
And his first one, which is very commonsensical and which has been strongly confirmed

192
01:29:46,120 --> 01:29:49,600
by our research is lower your expectations.

193
01:29:49,600 --> 01:29:52,040
Lower your expectations.

194
01:29:52,040 --> 01:29:55,880
Expect as little out of life as you possibly can and then you will be happier

195
01:29:55,880 --> 01:29:59,320
because you will appreciate what you have.

196
01:29:59,320 --> 01:30:04,480
All right, and many people, you know, many will hear this and then like, yeah,

197
01:30:04,480 --> 01:30:06,240
but I can't do that.

198
01:30:06,240 --> 01:30:08,520
Have you tried?

199
01:30:08,520 --> 01:30:09,720
Just try.

200
01:30:09,720 --> 01:30:13,680
At the margin, if you could just marginally lower your expectations by 5%,

201
01:30:13,680 --> 01:30:18,200
that would do more for your happiness than almost anything else you're trying to do right now.

202
01:30:18,200 --> 01:30:22,200
But I told this to my wife, she said, that's cheating.

203
01:30:22,200 --> 01:30:27,120
Well, who made up the rules of this game anyway?

204
01:30:27,120 --> 01:30:30,560
Who said those are the rules that you can't lower your expectations to be happy?

205
01:30:30,560 --> 01:30:35,720
Why not? All right, so in any case, utilitarianism is precisely

206
01:30:35,720 --> 01:30:40,680
about maximizing happiness or pleasure is another word that people sometimes use.

207
01:30:40,680 --> 01:30:43,320
And of course, there's a debate about whether happiness is just pleasure

208
01:30:43,320 --> 01:30:45,320
or whether there's more to it than that.

209
01:30:45,320 --> 01:30:51,040
Now, the main reason that economists rarely talk about happiness, although it has increased

210
01:30:51,040 --> 01:30:54,280
over time, is that talking about happiness seems

211
01:30:54,280 --> 01:30:57,160
to require interpersonal utility comparisons.

212
01:30:57,160 --> 01:30:58,280
Oh my God.

213
01:30:58,280 --> 01:31:02,760
Interpersonal utility comparisons, saying that one person is happier than another person

214
01:31:02,760 --> 01:31:06,360
and also figuring out how much happier they are than another person.

215
01:31:06,360 --> 01:31:09,440
So a way that some economists put it, how do you add happiness?

216
01:31:09,440 --> 01:31:10,720
How do you sum it?

217
01:31:10,720 --> 01:31:13,080
You know, if we were to say, what's the happiness of all the people on this side of the room

218
01:31:13,080 --> 01:31:16,400
and compare it to the happiness of all the people on that side of the room, how can we ever do that?

219
01:31:16,400 --> 01:31:20,360
It seems like a meaningless question, and so we shouldn't do it.

220
01:31:20,360 --> 01:31:23,640
Okay, so that is a common view among economists.

221
01:31:23,640 --> 01:31:29,640
Although, it is one that I've never known an economist to actually consistently execute.

222
01:31:29,640 --> 01:31:32,800
So even Walter Block.

223
01:31:32,800 --> 01:31:39,240
So as staunchly Austrian economist as I have ever met, who has an official position of,

224
01:31:39,240 --> 01:31:42,800
yeah, you can't do interpersonal comparisons at all.

225
01:31:42,800 --> 01:31:47,160
And I said, but like, what about like you here smiling versus that guy

226
01:31:47,160 --> 01:31:49,680
over there who seems really unhappy?

227
01:31:49,680 --> 01:31:54,520
And you're like, is it meaningless to say that you're happier than him?

228
01:31:54,520 --> 01:31:58,120
I mean, you might say that I'm wrong, but it just sort of doesn't seem meaningless.

229
01:31:58,120 --> 01:32:02,760
And then even he says, well, there's two senses of the word happiness.

230
01:32:02,760 --> 01:32:07,960
There's the normal sense, where sure, of course, I'm way happier than that guy over there.

231
01:32:07,960 --> 01:32:13,400
But then there's the scientific sense, in which case the sentence is meaningless.

232
01:32:13,400 --> 01:32:18,560
And then I said, well, could we just switch over to the normal meaning from now on?

233
01:32:18,560 --> 01:32:22,320
And then could we go and make the null meaning the scientific meaning

234
01:32:22,320 --> 01:32:27,000
and flush the scientific meaning down because it doesn't serve any purpose anyway?

235
01:32:27,000 --> 01:32:29,160
No. We're not allowed to do that.

236
01:32:29,160 --> 01:32:31,320
I'm still confused about why not.

237
01:32:31,320 --> 01:32:38,560
But in any case, so anyone who says that interpersonal utility comparisons are possible,

238
01:32:38,560 --> 01:32:41,920
yet they do actually rank people by more or less happy.

239
01:32:41,920 --> 01:32:44,920
They say that this friend is less happy than they normally are.

240
01:32:44,920 --> 01:32:47,760
They say that one friend is more happy than another person.

241
01:32:47,760 --> 01:32:51,400
They can go and say, look, who is the least happy character in Seinfeld?

242
01:32:51,400 --> 01:32:52,920
How do the main characters?

243
01:32:52,920 --> 01:32:54,200
Well, of course, it's George.

244
01:32:54,200 --> 01:32:57,960
The question's meaningless, but the answer is George.

245
01:32:57,960 --> 01:32:58,840
So they do this.

246
01:32:58,840 --> 01:33:06,200
And then, of course, once you start taking this philosophy or dogma, if you prefer, very seriously,

247
01:33:06,200 --> 01:33:11,720
then if you can't do interpersonal comparisons, why can you do intrapersonal comparisons?

248
01:33:11,720 --> 01:33:15,520
If it's meaningless to say that one person is happier than another, does it also mean

249
01:33:15,520 --> 01:33:19,480
that you can't say that one person is happier than he was yesterday?

250
01:33:19,480 --> 01:33:24,120
In both cases, if you say that you can't measure it so you can't compare it,

251
01:33:24,120 --> 01:33:28,560
then whether you're doing it across people or between people or even between yourself today

252
01:33:28,560 --> 01:33:34,240
and tomorrow, if you're at the point where you say, I can no longer say that I'm more

253
01:33:34,240 --> 01:33:37,720
or less happy than I was yesterday, it's a meaningless statement.

254
01:33:37,720 --> 01:33:38,200
All right?

255
01:33:38,200 --> 01:33:40,280
This is where the conversation ends.

256
01:33:40,280 --> 01:33:43,240
I'm not going to persuade you of anything, but doesn't that sound crazy?

257
01:33:43,240 --> 01:33:46,400
It seems crazy to me.

258
01:33:46,400 --> 01:33:46,760
All right.

259
01:33:46,760 --> 01:33:52,240
Now, some people who want to go and just be a little bit utilitarian will say, well, look,

260
01:33:52,240 --> 01:33:59,000
all utilitarianism is is like regular Calgary Hicks cost-benefit analysis with an adjustment

261
01:33:59,000 --> 01:34:00,600
for marginal utility and wealth.

262
01:34:00,600 --> 01:34:05,760
So all we do is the regular thing, and then we go and do an adjustment based upon the idea

263
01:34:05,760 --> 01:34:09,800
that a dollar's worth less to Bill Gates than it is to a homeless man.

264
01:34:09,800 --> 01:34:12,480
Okay? Of course, you know, at a philosophical level, that's no help

265
01:34:12,480 --> 01:34:14,360
because you saw it, so how much less?

266
01:34:14,360 --> 01:34:17,720
We can't do the calculation unless we go and assign a functional form.

267
01:34:17,720 --> 01:34:22,440
Is it like the square root of the wealth that is proportional to or what?

268
01:34:22,440 --> 01:34:27,040
But in any case, this is the usual problem where once you go and make one concession

269
01:34:27,040 --> 01:34:31,440
to common sense, then why not make two concessions to common sense?

270
01:34:31,440 --> 01:34:34,600
Why not make all the concessions to common sense?

271
01:34:34,600 --> 01:34:41,240
So once you say that you're factoring marginal utility wealth, why not also factor in things

272
01:34:41,240 --> 01:34:45,040
like some people are more materialistic than other people.

273
01:34:45,040 --> 01:34:49,080
Right? So more materialistic people get more joy out of material things.

274
01:34:49,080 --> 01:34:52,080
Less materialistic people get less joy out of material things.

275
01:34:52,080 --> 01:34:56,360
So if you're a utilitarian, who would you take money from?

276
01:34:56,360 --> 01:34:57,920
The monks.

277
01:34:57,920 --> 01:35:00,960
You take money from the monks, the people who don't care about material things,

278
01:35:00,960 --> 01:35:04,480
who don't appreciate material things, and then you give it to the yuppies.

279
01:35:04,480 --> 01:35:08,480
You give it to the people who like shopping for designer goods and the people

280
01:35:08,480 --> 01:35:11,440
who have a big smile when they buy a new pair of pants.

281
01:35:11,440 --> 01:35:15,760
You know, we took money from those monks who don't care about material possessions at all

282
01:35:15,760 --> 01:35:19,000
and gave it to the people who just love talking about, well,

283
01:35:19,000 --> 01:35:22,240
this pair of designer jeans might seem no better than the other.

284
01:35:22,240 --> 01:35:23,320
Oh, but you're so wrong.

285
01:35:23,320 --> 01:35:24,560
It's so much better.

286
01:35:24,560 --> 01:35:25,120
It's wonderful.

287
01:35:25,120 --> 01:35:26,920
The stitching, look at it.

288
01:35:26,920 --> 01:35:27,760
It's wonderful.

289
01:35:27,760 --> 01:35:30,240
Right? And those people I have met, actually.

290
01:35:30,240 --> 01:35:31,840
Not the great people.

291
01:35:31,840 --> 01:35:36,280
But the great lovers are, as far as you can tell, just non-existent.

292
01:35:36,280 --> 01:35:38,520
They've got to be out there somewhere.

293
01:35:38,520 --> 01:35:41,800
But I've looked around and I know a lot of people with granite.

294
01:35:41,800 --> 01:35:44,640
But the people who can get excited over clothes, I've seen those.

295
01:35:44,640 --> 01:35:46,480
Those people are out there.

296
01:35:46,480 --> 01:35:47,040
All right.

297
01:35:47,040 --> 01:35:52,120
So once you start saying, well, yeah, it's not just about marginal utility of wealth.

298
01:35:52,120 --> 01:35:54,920
We have to adjust for how materialistic you are.

299
01:35:54,920 --> 01:35:59,080
And then you might want to just, like, how happy of a person are you?

300
01:35:59,080 --> 01:36:04,880
So you might think that people who just have a happy personality just appreciate everything more.

301
01:36:04,880 --> 01:36:08,040
Yeah, it's possible that miserable people have higher marginal utility.

302
01:36:08,040 --> 01:36:10,480
But I've never seen any sign of it.

303
01:36:10,480 --> 01:36:13,680
So, you know, like the perennial miserable person, you get them a present.

304
01:36:13,680 --> 01:36:14,760
Oh, thanks.

305
01:36:14,760 --> 01:36:17,240
See, but it's not just the material thing.

306
01:36:17,240 --> 01:36:18,960
It also shows people care about you.

307
01:36:18,960 --> 01:36:19,960
Yes, they care about me.

308
01:36:19,960 --> 01:36:21,360
That's so great.

309
01:36:21,360 --> 01:36:22,280
All right.

310
01:36:22,280 --> 01:36:22,560
All right.

311
01:36:22,560 --> 01:36:23,840
So you know some people like this.

312
01:36:23,840 --> 01:36:27,680
And then there's people, oh, goody, a present for me.

313
01:36:27,680 --> 01:36:28,080
All right.

314
01:36:28,080 --> 01:36:33,080
So, you know, if you're a utilitarian, want to transfer resources from the grumpy people

315
01:36:33,080 --> 01:36:37,040
over to the cheerful people who will really appreciate the stuff.

316
01:36:37,040 --> 01:36:37,720
All right.

317
01:36:37,720 --> 01:36:40,480
So that is what would be applied by utilitarian.

318
01:36:40,480 --> 01:36:47,120
And once you make the adjusting for wealth, then why not adjust for everything you can think of?

319
01:36:47,120 --> 01:36:48,080
All right.

320
01:36:48,080 --> 01:36:52,880
So anyway, so people often say utilitarianism is factors of marginal utility of wealth.

321
01:36:52,880 --> 01:36:54,280
But not necessarily true.

322
01:36:54,280 --> 01:36:56,920
People might be willing to pay for things other than happiness.

323
01:36:56,920 --> 01:37:00,360
And utilitarianism is often used to justify redistribution.

324
01:37:00,360 --> 01:37:02,560
But even on its own terms, it's not necessarily followed.

325
01:37:02,560 --> 01:37:06,040
There's a famous counter example involving a utility monster.

326
01:37:06,040 --> 01:37:09,680
This is a creature that has increasing marginal utility of wealth.

327
01:37:09,680 --> 01:37:13,040
Even if you don't think they currently exist, they could be genetically engineered.

328
01:37:13,040 --> 01:37:17,320
And if you were utilitarian, then you'd like, let's just go and give everything

329
01:37:17,320 --> 01:37:21,360
to the utility monster who just loves stuff beyond all imagination,

330
01:37:21,360 --> 01:37:23,920
more than any of us could possibly do.

331
01:37:23,920 --> 01:37:25,360
All right.

332
01:37:25,360 --> 01:37:27,520
So questions?

333
01:37:27,520 --> 01:37:28,480
Questions?

334
01:37:28,480 --> 01:37:31,640
So this, by the way, is the most philosophical lecture by far.

335
01:37:31,640 --> 01:37:34,920
Although I mention it because it's so interesting and worth knowing.

336
01:37:34,920 --> 01:37:39,320
And also, GMU PhDs are especially likely to do economics and philosophy.

337
01:37:39,320 --> 01:37:44,280
So perhaps I am starting you on your pathway to these bigger and better things.

338
01:37:44,280 --> 01:37:47,440
And note, if you do want to do philosophy, the best place to do it is in the econ department

339
01:37:47,440 --> 01:37:48,840
where you have a job and a salary.

340
01:37:48,840 --> 01:37:56,840
So just, you may need to do something else in addition until you have tenure.

341
01:37:56,840 --> 01:38:04,680
And then, it's all philosophy now, with economics in the background.

342
01:38:04,680 --> 01:38:06,960
So moving on.

343
01:38:06,960 --> 01:38:07,160
All right.

344
01:38:07,160 --> 01:38:10,480
The Commercial Instant Proofs approach and second best.

345
01:38:10,480 --> 01:38:16,360
So I'm guessing that Walter Williams told you about Harold Dempset at some point.

346
01:38:16,360 --> 01:38:18,320
Yes, the great Harold Dempset.

347
01:38:18,320 --> 01:38:23,200
So Dempset famously complained about what he called the Nirvana Fallacy.

348
01:38:23,200 --> 01:38:28,040
All right, which, as far as I understand, is based upon a misunderstanding of Buddhist

349
01:38:28,040 --> 01:38:28,600
theology.

350
01:38:28,600 --> 01:38:32,800
But almost everybody misunderstands it in the same way.

351
01:38:32,800 --> 01:38:36,200
So he still makes his point.

352
01:38:36,200 --> 01:38:40,800
All right, so what is the Nirvana Fallacy?

353
01:38:40,800 --> 01:38:43,840
Metaphorically, it's comparing the real world to heaven.

354
01:38:43,840 --> 01:38:47,000
From what I understand, Nirvana is actually a state of nothingness, not heaven.

355
01:38:47,000 --> 01:38:51,640
But in any case, comparing the real world to an unrealizable ideal.

356
01:38:51,640 --> 01:38:54,720
And then say, you know what, the real world stinks compared to heaven.

357
01:38:54,720 --> 01:38:59,760
And so now, let's go and regulate pollution.

358
01:38:59,760 --> 01:39:00,400
All right.

359
01:39:00,400 --> 01:39:04,920
So anyway, what he literally meant was doing efficiency calculations.

360
01:39:04,920 --> 01:39:08,480
And again, of course, since he's talking about real world calculations,

361
01:39:08,480 --> 01:39:10,600
he's talking about Caldeirx or cost benefits.

362
01:39:10,600 --> 01:39:14,480
So doing Caldeirx efficiency comparisons while selectively relaxing

363
01:39:14,480 --> 01:39:16,440
important constraints.

364
01:39:16,440 --> 01:39:18,920
While selectively relaxing important constraints, generally without ever

365
01:39:18,920 --> 01:39:22,240
mentioning you're doing it, and just hoping everyone in the audience will let

366
01:39:22,240 --> 01:39:26,360
you get away with it, because they have the same bias of what they're looking for.

367
01:39:26,360 --> 01:39:28,480
All right, so what in the world is going on?

368
01:39:28,480 --> 01:39:34,760
Well, Demsetz is criticizing old style welfare economics of the 1950s, the 1960s,

369
01:39:34,760 --> 01:39:39,520
1970s, and based upon what it was like when I was an undergraduate, also the 1980s

370
01:39:39,520 --> 01:39:41,480
and the 1990s.

371
01:39:41,480 --> 01:39:45,480
And how does old style welfare economics works?

372
01:39:45,480 --> 01:39:46,680
Well, it's something like this.

373
01:39:46,680 --> 01:39:51,040
Step one, you point out one of these deadweight loss triangles.

374
01:39:51,040 --> 01:39:51,760
All right.

375
01:39:51,760 --> 01:39:56,400
Step two, you say, government can, should, will, and must fix that deadweight loss

376
01:39:56,400 --> 01:39:57,600
triangle.

377
01:39:57,600 --> 01:40:17,600
And then say, and that's it.


1
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:00,840
End of story.

2
01:40:00,840 --> 01:40:03,160
All right, and you say, what's the problem with that?

3
01:40:03,160 --> 01:40:05,240
Well, there might be some cost

4
01:40:05,240 --> 01:40:07,160
of fixing that Nedley-Loss Triangle.

5
01:40:07,160 --> 01:40:09,120
Like at minimum, there's gonna be the cost

6
01:40:09,120 --> 01:40:10,320
of paying the bureaucrats

7
01:40:10,320 --> 01:40:12,880
and having their bureau building, right?

8
01:40:12,880 --> 01:40:13,720
If you walk around D.C.,

9
01:40:13,720 --> 01:40:15,080
there's all these bureau buildings.

10
01:40:15,080 --> 01:40:17,840
Those resources have to come from somewhere.

11
01:40:17,840 --> 01:40:21,020
So suppose that the cost of fixing

12
01:40:21,020 --> 01:40:24,360
that Nedley-Loss Triangle was that much

13
01:40:24,360 --> 01:40:26,280
in bureaucrats and buildings.

14
01:40:26,280 --> 01:40:27,860
It would not make sense to go

15
01:40:27,860 --> 01:40:30,600
and have government go and fix

16
01:40:30,600 --> 01:40:32,800
and spend that much in resources

17
01:40:32,800 --> 01:40:35,280
to fix that much in losses, right?

18
01:40:35,280 --> 01:40:37,340
That would be, technical term, stupid

19
01:40:37,340 --> 01:40:40,880
because you are, if the loss is relatively small,

20
01:40:40,880 --> 01:40:43,040
the cost of fixing it is much higher.

21
01:40:43,040 --> 01:40:44,700
It is rather more economically efficient

22
01:40:44,700 --> 01:40:47,000
to live with the problem, right?

23
01:40:47,000 --> 01:40:49,680
And could people really be so dumb?

24
01:40:49,680 --> 01:40:52,400
Well, I had a lot of undergraduate courses

25
01:40:52,400 --> 01:40:54,280
where professors who knew the real world

26
01:40:54,280 --> 01:40:57,080
would go and put these diagrams on there

27
01:40:57,080 --> 01:40:59,360
and then say, and what is the solution?

28
01:40:59,360 --> 01:41:01,000
Not what could be the solution,

29
01:41:01,000 --> 01:41:02,740
what might be the solution if the cost is low,

30
01:41:02,740 --> 01:41:03,940
but what is the solution

31
01:41:03,940 --> 01:41:06,640
and the solution is government intervention.

32
01:41:06,640 --> 01:41:08,840
And very often the specifics,

33
01:41:08,840 --> 01:41:10,400
the details of the intervention

34
01:41:10,400 --> 01:41:12,400
were so wildly impractical,

35
01:41:12,400 --> 01:41:14,280
you could never do it in the real world,

36
01:41:14,280 --> 01:41:15,440
as we shall see.

37
01:41:15,440 --> 01:41:16,700
And then you're really wondering,

38
01:41:16,700 --> 01:41:18,880
so there's a problem here

39
01:41:18,880 --> 01:41:21,800
and your proposed solution is completely undoable

40
01:41:21,800 --> 01:41:24,240
and that's an argument for government, how?

41
01:41:24,240 --> 01:41:27,200
Just like, wait, is this an argument for government

42
01:41:27,200 --> 01:41:30,000
in a homework problem or in the real world?

43
01:41:30,000 --> 01:41:33,240
And the tone was always, well, of course, in the real world,

44
01:41:33,240 --> 01:41:35,840
but the answer only applied in a homework problem.

45
01:41:37,280 --> 01:41:39,680
So, shortcomings of government according to Demsets

46
01:41:39,680 --> 01:41:41,320
and I think this is a very fair description

47
01:41:41,320 --> 01:41:43,800
of the welfare economics of his era, right?

48
01:41:43,800 --> 01:41:46,480
And even basic overhead almost never factored in.

49
01:41:46,480 --> 01:41:50,680
So, classic example of the intervention policy.

50
01:41:50,680 --> 01:41:52,680
We go and we take a look at something like this,

51
01:41:52,680 --> 01:41:54,480
we have a monopoly and we see the price

52
01:41:54,480 --> 01:41:56,280
is greater than marginal cost.

53
01:41:57,520 --> 01:41:59,160
Right, in fact, we have,

54
01:41:59,160 --> 01:42:00,800
probably this is where marginal revenue

55
01:42:00,800 --> 01:42:02,200
equals marginal cost, whatever that's.

56
01:42:02,200 --> 01:42:03,480
And so you've all seen

57
01:42:03,480 --> 01:42:05,500
deadweight loss of monopoly at some point.

58
01:42:05,500 --> 01:42:07,440
All right, so we're right here,

59
01:42:07,440 --> 01:42:09,200
leading to this deadweight loss.

60
01:42:09,200 --> 01:42:12,120
All right, now, the textbook solution

61
01:42:12,120 --> 01:42:14,280
is just to impose a price control

62
01:42:14,280 --> 01:42:16,740
set requiring price to be equal to marginal cost.

63
01:42:17,760 --> 01:42:19,920
Right, and wait, how come your price controls don't work?

64
01:42:19,920 --> 01:42:21,720
Well, if there's a monopoly

65
01:42:21,720 --> 01:42:24,800
set up exactly like this, price controls work great.

66
01:42:24,800 --> 01:42:27,360
So, if the world is exactly like this,

67
01:42:27,360 --> 01:42:29,120
then all we do is say, well,

68
01:42:29,120 --> 01:42:31,040
price must be equal to that.

69
01:42:31,040 --> 01:42:32,680
And if price must be equal to that,

70
01:42:32,680 --> 01:42:36,040
then the monopolist maximizes profits

71
01:42:36,040 --> 01:42:38,480
just by going and producing equal to marginal cost

72
01:42:38,480 --> 01:42:40,280
and problem solved.

73
01:42:40,280 --> 01:42:42,800
Okay, now this immediately gives rise

74
01:42:42,800 --> 01:42:44,340
in the real world to a secondary problem,

75
01:42:44,340 --> 01:42:48,480
which is that all real world monopolies have fixed costs.

76
01:42:48,480 --> 01:42:51,240
So, if you make firms charge a price equal to marginal cost

77
01:42:51,240 --> 01:42:54,560
and there are fixed costs, then all firms lose money.

78
01:42:54,560 --> 01:42:57,520
And therefore, the regulations effect

79
01:42:57,520 --> 01:42:59,520
will not be to reduce the price of the product,

80
01:42:59,520 --> 01:43:02,280
but rather to destroy the existence of the product,

81
01:43:02,280 --> 01:43:04,160
which is not the intention, right?

82
01:43:04,160 --> 01:43:05,280
So, the goal of the price control

83
01:43:05,280 --> 01:43:07,040
was not to eliminate the product from existence,

84
01:43:07,040 --> 01:43:09,880
it was to eliminate that deadweight loss.

85
01:43:09,880 --> 01:43:11,880
All right, now, then of course,

86
01:43:11,880 --> 01:43:12,960
there's a standard solution to that.

87
01:43:12,960 --> 01:43:15,440
Well, we'll just go and subsidize firms

88
01:43:15,440 --> 01:43:16,760
at amount equal to their fixed cost

89
01:43:16,760 --> 01:43:18,680
and that will keep them in business.

90
01:43:18,680 --> 01:43:19,880
So, we have the price control

91
01:43:19,880 --> 01:43:21,600
and then we have the subsidy to cover the fixed costs

92
01:43:21,600 --> 01:43:23,560
and now everything's great, right?

93
01:43:23,560 --> 01:43:25,320
Well, then there is a tertiary problem.

94
01:43:25,320 --> 01:43:27,840
How are we gonna fund these subsidies?

95
01:43:27,840 --> 01:43:30,480
With lump sum taxes.

96
01:43:31,600 --> 01:43:33,660
All right, and which are the countries on earth

97
01:43:33,660 --> 01:43:35,320
that have lump sum taxes again?

98
01:43:36,680 --> 01:43:37,720
Which are, where are they?

99
01:43:37,720 --> 01:43:38,640
Which ones are these?

100
01:43:38,640 --> 01:43:42,060
There are none, as far as I know, zero countries like this.

101
01:43:42,060 --> 01:43:43,360
So, in the real world,

102
01:43:43,360 --> 01:43:46,660
well, like what kind of taxes would pay for the subsidies?

103
01:43:46,660 --> 01:43:48,520
So, for almost all countries,

104
01:43:48,520 --> 01:43:51,360
these would be your usual disturbance of taxes.

105
01:43:51,360 --> 01:43:52,920
And remember, what is the essence

106
01:43:52,920 --> 01:43:54,400
of what a tax assertion does?

107
01:43:54,400 --> 01:43:55,600
No, what a tax assertion,

108
01:43:55,600 --> 01:43:57,640
what is the essence of what a tax assertion does?

109
01:43:57,640 --> 01:44:01,880
It is to raise price above marginal cost.

110
01:44:01,880 --> 01:44:05,800
It is to create a wedge between the value that is received

111
01:44:05,800 --> 01:44:07,720
by the person delivering the product

112
01:44:07,720 --> 01:44:10,300
and the amount they are paid.

113
01:44:10,300 --> 01:44:14,760
So, you produce more value than you actually receive,

114
01:44:14,760 --> 01:44:16,700
which then leads again to this deadweight cost.

115
01:44:16,700 --> 01:44:19,560
In fact, if you estimate the deadweight cost of taxation,

116
01:44:19,560 --> 01:44:21,440
you also get a triangle.

117
01:44:21,440 --> 01:44:24,500
It is exactly the same geometrically.

118
01:44:25,600 --> 01:44:28,640
All right, so then what about the deadweight cost of taxes?

119
01:44:28,640 --> 01:44:29,640
What about those?

120
01:44:30,520 --> 01:44:32,280
All right, and then notice I haven't even covered

121
01:44:32,280 --> 01:44:33,680
some of the other obvious problems,

122
01:44:33,680 --> 01:44:36,400
like what is marginal cost, what are fixed costs,

123
01:44:36,400 --> 01:44:38,640
how would you discover what they are?

124
01:44:38,640 --> 01:44:39,760
Are you just gonna ask people?

125
01:44:39,760 --> 01:44:41,440
If you do, what are your fixed costs, man?

126
01:44:41,440 --> 01:44:43,640
Because I'm giving you a subsidy, which equals something.

127
01:44:43,640 --> 01:44:47,600
You could do that that's not a very effective method.

128
01:44:47,600 --> 01:44:50,320
At least that won't drive firms out of business.

129
01:44:50,320 --> 01:44:52,720
They'll all stay in business once you do that.

130
01:44:53,760 --> 01:44:55,340
Maybe they might be killed by the taxes

131
01:44:55,340 --> 01:44:56,680
that have to be paid in order to pay

132
01:44:56,680 --> 01:44:58,400
for the subsidies for everybody else.

133
01:44:58,400 --> 01:45:02,200
But still, but then you might say,

134
01:45:02,200 --> 01:45:03,840
no, let's go and have a panel to figure out

135
01:45:03,840 --> 01:45:05,360
what your fixed costs are.

136
01:45:05,360 --> 01:45:08,360
And so that creates problems as well.

137
01:45:08,360 --> 01:45:10,280
So maybe, so if the number's too high,

138
01:45:10,280 --> 01:45:13,400
then you are going and imposing unnecessary deadweight costs

139
01:45:13,400 --> 01:45:14,760
for excessive taxation.

140
01:45:14,760 --> 01:45:16,360
If it's lower, then again, you just wind up

141
01:45:16,360 --> 01:45:20,040
destroying the firms that you are regulating.

142
01:45:20,040 --> 01:45:21,720
Right, and then strategically, of course,

143
01:45:21,720 --> 01:45:23,720
if you run one of these firms,

144
01:45:23,720 --> 01:45:26,280
you might want to pretend to die in order to say,

145
01:45:26,280 --> 01:45:29,280
oh, I'm about to die if only my subsidy

146
01:45:29,280 --> 01:45:31,280
would not have to cover my fixed costs.

147
01:45:31,280 --> 01:45:33,440
Then I could continue supplying food to the children.

148
01:45:33,440 --> 01:45:36,480
But you can't do that because the subsidies

149
01:45:36,480 --> 01:45:37,680
won't remain sufficient.

150
01:45:37,680 --> 01:45:41,200
All right, and then you're having to fire all of my employees

151
01:45:41,200 --> 01:45:43,440
except for me now.

152
01:45:43,440 --> 01:45:46,600
And the subsidy, you can fight in your heart

153
01:45:46,600 --> 01:45:48,200
to go and double the subsidy,

154
01:45:48,200 --> 01:45:49,680
and maybe I could come back from this.

155
01:45:49,680 --> 01:45:50,920
So you could try that.

156
01:45:52,520 --> 01:45:54,480
So why does Dems test his alternative

157
01:45:54,480 --> 01:45:56,280
to the crummy welfare economics

158
01:45:56,280 --> 01:45:59,400
that almost everyone by himself was doing at the time?

159
01:46:00,280 --> 01:46:02,000
Well, what he said is that economists should use

160
01:46:02,000 --> 01:46:04,520
what he called a comparative institutions approach,

161
01:46:04,520 --> 01:46:06,320
which begins with the motto,

162
01:46:06,320 --> 01:46:08,720
nothing is perfectly efficient.

163
01:46:08,720 --> 01:46:12,120
We never are going to be in a perfectly counter,

164
01:46:12,120 --> 01:46:14,400
in a perfectly counter exhibition world.

165
01:46:14,400 --> 01:46:16,120
So instead, we should be asking

166
01:46:16,120 --> 01:46:18,200
what out of all the feasible options,

167
01:46:18,200 --> 01:46:21,520
which one gives us the lowest deadweight costs?

168
01:46:21,520 --> 01:46:23,720
Which one gives, which one leaves,

169
01:46:23,720 --> 01:46:26,760
makes us closest to the efficient outcome?

170
01:46:26,760 --> 01:46:31,760
Right, and associated with this approach is a,

171
01:46:31,920 --> 01:46:32,760
what is it called?

172
01:46:32,760 --> 01:46:35,000
A, where's the word for it again?

173
01:46:35,880 --> 01:46:37,480
An allegory, that's an allegory.

174
01:46:37,480 --> 01:46:40,760
So an allegory is a story where each of the characters

175
01:46:40,760 --> 01:46:44,760
and events stands for some other characters and events.

176
01:46:44,760 --> 01:46:47,520
So you know George Orwell's Animal Farm, it's an allegory.

177
01:46:47,520 --> 01:46:49,480
Napoleon is Joseph Stalin.

178
01:46:49,480 --> 01:46:51,440
Snowball is Leon Trotsky.

179
01:46:51,440 --> 01:46:54,400
The Raven is Rasputin, supposedly, and so on.

180
01:46:54,400 --> 01:46:56,520
All right, so, and like the windmill getting blown up

181
01:46:56,520 --> 01:46:58,240
the second time is World War II.

182
01:46:58,240 --> 01:47:00,720
Right, so if you go and have your cliff notes

183
01:47:00,720 --> 01:47:01,560
and you read through it,

184
01:47:01,560 --> 01:47:03,280
you will map this correspondence

185
01:47:03,280 --> 01:47:04,640
between the characters and events

186
01:47:04,640 --> 01:47:07,840
and other real world characters and events.

187
01:47:07,840 --> 01:47:11,480
So anyway, the Tale of the Emperor is an allegory

188
01:47:11,480 --> 01:47:12,800
and it goes like this.

189
01:47:12,800 --> 01:47:15,840
The Emperor of Rome announces a great musical contest

190
01:47:15,840 --> 01:47:18,360
to find the best musician in the entire empire.

191
01:47:19,360 --> 01:47:21,360
Only two musicians show up.

192
01:47:21,360 --> 01:47:24,000
The first musician begins to play, but he misses a note.

193
01:47:24,000 --> 01:47:27,020
So the Emperor says the contest is over.

194
01:47:27,020 --> 01:47:29,160
The second musician is the greatest in the realm

195
01:47:29,160 --> 01:47:30,520
and he has the prize.

196
01:47:30,520 --> 01:47:31,360
The end.

197
01:47:32,400 --> 01:47:33,880
That's the allegory.

198
01:47:33,880 --> 01:47:36,040
All right, so how is it an allegory?

199
01:47:36,040 --> 01:47:37,880
Well, the Roman Emperor is supposed to be

200
01:47:37,880 --> 01:47:39,880
the mainstream economics profession.

201
01:47:39,880 --> 01:47:41,680
The first musician who misses one note

202
01:47:41,680 --> 01:47:43,120
is supposed to be the free market.

203
01:47:43,120 --> 01:47:45,840
And the second musician that we haven't even bothered

204
01:47:45,840 --> 01:47:47,320
looking at and listening to how he plays

205
01:47:47,320 --> 01:47:49,400
is supposed to be government.

206
01:47:49,400 --> 01:47:52,440
And this allegory is a criticism of welfare economics

207
01:47:52,440 --> 01:47:57,080
of the 50s and 60s and 70s for economists

208
01:47:57,080 --> 01:47:59,280
going and finding any fault they can in free markets

209
01:47:59,280 --> 01:48:01,720
and then saying that government can, will, should,

210
01:48:01,720 --> 01:48:03,640
and must fix it without ever bothering

211
01:48:03,640 --> 01:48:05,640
to actually investigate how government really works

212
01:48:05,640 --> 01:48:06,560
in the real world.

213
01:48:06,560 --> 01:48:08,400
And of course, there's a very strong public choice theme

214
01:48:08,400 --> 01:48:09,920
here of saying, why don't we look at how government

215
01:48:09,920 --> 01:48:11,880
actually functions?

216
01:48:11,880 --> 01:48:14,200
I remember when I was the TA

217
01:48:14,200 --> 01:48:16,140
for Industrial Organization of Princeton,

218
01:48:16,140 --> 01:48:18,560
the professor was, young assistant professor,

219
01:48:18,560 --> 01:48:20,480
only knew models, only knew theory,

220
01:48:20,480 --> 01:48:23,720
knew no empirics, knew no history of any kind.

221
01:48:23,720 --> 01:48:26,560
So when he was teaching antitrust,

222
01:48:26,560 --> 01:48:28,700
I would occasionally, anytime possible,

223
01:48:28,700 --> 01:48:31,120
bring up actual antitrust cases that were relevant,

224
01:48:31,120 --> 01:48:33,120
pointing out how the actual antitrust cases

225
01:48:33,120 --> 01:48:36,680
had been often did the opposite of what he said

226
01:48:36,680 --> 01:48:38,080
would be a good idea.

227
01:48:38,080 --> 01:48:39,360
And finally said, you know, Brian,

228
01:48:39,360 --> 01:48:41,760
I don't care about any of the history of antitrust.

229
01:48:41,760 --> 01:48:44,840
All that matters is what policy,

230
01:48:44,840 --> 01:48:46,880
all that matters is just giving the best policy advice

231
01:48:46,880 --> 01:48:50,000
to the government so it can actually do a good job.

232
01:48:50,000 --> 01:48:53,080
And I said, well, but shouldn't we look

233
01:48:53,080 --> 01:48:54,760
at the past history of antitrust to get an idea

234
01:48:54,760 --> 01:48:56,760
about what government is actually likely to do

235
01:48:56,760 --> 01:48:58,200
and how the extent of government

236
01:48:58,200 --> 01:49:00,480
is actually likely to listen to good advice?

237
01:49:00,480 --> 01:49:01,920
And then he said, well, by that standard,

238
01:49:01,920 --> 01:49:03,480
we would never have any governments.

239
01:49:03,480 --> 01:49:07,520
And I said, that's way stronger than I would have said.

240
01:49:07,520 --> 01:49:10,200
But anyway, it's a good concession.

241
01:49:11,040 --> 01:49:11,880
Yes.

242
01:49:11,880 --> 01:49:12,700
Who was the professor?

243
01:49:12,700 --> 01:49:13,800
I'm not gonna name names.

244
01:49:13,800 --> 01:49:17,280
He eventually did some empirics and got tenure.

245
01:49:17,280 --> 01:49:19,400
But not a great story.

246
01:49:19,400 --> 01:49:23,160
All right, so that's the tale of the emperor.

247
01:49:23,160 --> 01:49:25,280
Now when you add more constraints to a standard problem,

248
01:49:25,280 --> 01:49:27,760
the original optimum is no longer feasible.

249
01:49:27,760 --> 01:49:29,160
And it's very common for economists

250
01:49:29,160 --> 01:49:32,000
to call the original optimum with very few constraints,

251
01:49:32,000 --> 01:49:33,560
the first best optimum,

252
01:49:33,560 --> 01:49:35,440
and the optimum with the additional constraints

253
01:49:35,440 --> 01:49:37,560
as a second best optimum.

254
01:49:37,560 --> 01:49:39,280
So it's common, for example,

255
01:49:39,280 --> 01:49:41,080
to say that setting price equal to marginal cost

256
01:49:41,080 --> 01:49:42,560
is the first best optimum,

257
01:49:42,560 --> 01:49:45,480
setting price equal to average cost is the second best.

258
01:49:45,480 --> 01:49:48,380
So markets will often give you average cost pricing,

259
01:49:48,380 --> 01:49:51,520
but very rarely will they give you marginal cost pricing.

260
01:49:51,520 --> 01:49:53,880
And intuitively, think about the route

261
01:49:53,880 --> 01:49:55,240
that you drove to school today.

262
01:49:55,240 --> 01:49:56,360
Now imagine what your route would be

263
01:49:56,360 --> 01:50:00,000
if you had to stop by Kentucky Fried Chicken on the way.

264
01:50:00,000 --> 01:50:02,260
It is very likely that that would be a different route

265
01:50:02,260 --> 01:50:04,600
when you add on that extra constraint.

266
01:50:04,600 --> 01:50:05,960
Unless by sheer coincidence,

267
01:50:05,960 --> 01:50:07,360
you already are driving right past

268
01:50:07,360 --> 01:50:08,520
Kentucky Fried Chicken.

269
01:50:09,400 --> 01:50:11,360
In general, any time you add additional constraints

270
01:50:11,360 --> 01:50:12,880
to an optimization problem,

271
01:50:12,880 --> 01:50:15,160
it changes the result and gives you something

272
01:50:15,160 --> 01:50:19,760
that is harder to do because you added on more constraints.

273
01:50:19,760 --> 01:50:22,360
And that is the idea of why adding on more constraints

274
01:50:22,360 --> 01:50:24,140
goes and changes the results.

275
01:50:24,140 --> 01:50:25,240
So sometimes people talk about,

276
01:50:25,240 --> 01:50:28,080
what if you add in public choice constraints, for example?

277
01:50:28,080 --> 01:50:29,720
How does that change the optimum?

278
01:50:29,720 --> 01:50:31,400
And every now and then, economists will talk about

279
01:50:31,400 --> 01:50:33,760
a policy being 15th best.

280
01:50:33,760 --> 01:50:36,480
This is where it's so weighed down with the baggage,

281
01:50:36,480 --> 01:50:38,600
the real world, it looks horrifying.

282
01:50:38,600 --> 01:50:41,280
But where you say, look, given the fact that

283
01:50:41,280 --> 01:50:43,280
we couldn't get anything unless

284
01:50:43,280 --> 01:50:46,600
congressmen from Colorado got a bow and arrow subsidy,

285
01:50:46,600 --> 01:50:50,840
and unless Haiti got a new water slide,

286
01:50:50,840 --> 01:50:53,160
and unless we declared war on one country,

287
01:50:53,160 --> 01:50:54,520
didn't matter which one,

288
01:50:54,520 --> 01:50:57,320
and unless we did all these things,

289
01:50:57,320 --> 01:50:58,800
that's the only way to get to this deal,

290
01:50:58,800 --> 01:51:00,660
so it's 15th best.

291
01:51:00,660 --> 01:51:01,500
Sorry.

292
01:51:01,500 --> 01:51:03,360
People will sometimes say that.

293
01:51:04,240 --> 01:51:05,320
All right.

294
01:51:05,320 --> 01:51:08,080
So, questions, questions?

295
01:51:08,080 --> 01:51:08,920
All right.

296
01:51:08,920 --> 01:51:11,160
So, now, big question.

297
01:51:11,160 --> 01:51:12,240
Who cares about efficiency anyway?

298
01:51:12,240 --> 01:51:15,380
We spent now two hours talking about efficiency,

299
01:51:15,380 --> 01:51:17,240
and many people say, well,

300
01:51:17,240 --> 01:51:18,880
what difference does it make anyway?

301
01:51:18,880 --> 01:51:19,720
It's like saying, you know,

302
01:51:19,720 --> 01:51:21,240
some concepts you came up from homework problems,

303
01:51:21,240 --> 01:51:22,840
maybe you can, like the second one,

304
01:51:22,840 --> 01:51:24,600
at least apply to the real world,

305
01:51:24,600 --> 01:51:28,400
but we could have gone and we could come up

306
01:51:28,400 --> 01:51:29,400
with a new efficiency standard,

307
01:51:29,400 --> 01:51:31,480
say, you know what, we should maximize

308
01:51:31,480 --> 01:51:33,880
like the number of trees,

309
01:51:33,880 --> 01:51:36,380
or the number of copies of Brian's books.

310
01:51:36,380 --> 01:51:37,920
That's what society should be all about.

311
01:51:37,920 --> 01:51:39,800
We could go and analyze everything by

312
01:51:39,800 --> 01:51:42,520
Kaplanian book maximization efficiency,

313
01:51:42,520 --> 01:51:43,340
and then we'd say, like,

314
01:51:43,340 --> 01:51:45,560
how can we squeeze more of his books out of the system?

315
01:51:45,560 --> 01:51:46,400
How can we do that?

