01:61:00,880 --> 01:61:02,880
In fact, there probably were a bunch of Adolf Hitlers.

25
01:61:02,880 --> 01:61:05,880
There's a great documentary called Meet the Hitlers,

26
01:61:05,880 --> 01:61:07,880
about a bunch of people whose name is Hitler,

27
01:61:07,880 --> 01:61:09,880
and why they've not changed their name.

28
01:61:10,880 --> 01:61:13,880
Most are not Nazi sympathizers at all.

29
01:61:13,880 --> 01:61:15,880
Most of them are just people saying,

30
01:61:15,880 --> 01:61:17,880
I was born a Hitler, why should I have to change my name,

31
01:61:17,880 --> 01:61:19,880
just because there was one bad Hitler?

32
01:61:19,880 --> 01:61:22,880
Yes?

33
01:61:22,880 --> 01:61:25,880
How can we know how much them not being Nazis is social desirability bias,

34
01:61:25,880 --> 01:61:27,880
rather than actual use?

35
01:61:27,880 --> 01:61:29,880
Yeah, that's a great question.

36
01:61:29,880 --> 01:61:33,880
So, interesting possibility.

37
01:61:33,880 --> 01:61:38,880
So, one of them is just a teenage girl from North Carolina,

38
01:61:38,880 --> 01:61:40,880
she doesn't seem to know anything about it.

39
01:61:40,880 --> 01:61:42,880
I guess she could be hiding, but...

40
01:61:42,880 --> 01:61:44,880
I mean, normally they were born with a name.

41
01:61:44,880 --> 01:61:50,880
So, if your last name was Manson, would you change it,

42
01:61:50,880 --> 01:61:51,880
just because it's Charles Manson?

43
01:61:51,880 --> 01:61:56,880
One bad Manson shouldn't ruin the name for every Manson in the world.

44
01:61:56,880 --> 01:61:59,880
And, you know, that seems reasonable.

45
01:61:59,880 --> 01:62:01,880
There's plenty of Mansons out there, perfectly fine folk.

46
01:62:01,880 --> 01:62:03,880
Why should they have to change their name?

47
01:62:03,880 --> 01:62:06,880
But yes, it could be that they are just there,

48
01:62:06,880 --> 01:62:09,880
claiming that they don't want to change their name,

49
01:62:09,880 --> 01:62:12,880
because they like the name, but actually they're just Nazis.

50
01:62:12,880 --> 01:62:14,880
There's one guy who definitely is a Nazi,

51
01:62:14,880 --> 01:62:16,880
he's got a bunch of swastikas on him,

52
01:62:16,880 --> 01:62:18,880
and, well, he named his kids.

53
01:62:18,880 --> 01:62:20,880
One of them is like, I can't remember, but...

54
01:62:20,880 --> 01:62:24,880
But it's like, there's more to it than that.

55
01:62:24,880 --> 01:62:26,880
It's like Adolf Himmler,

56
01:62:26,880 --> 01:62:29,880
you know, you change the spelling to make it more creative.

57
01:62:29,880 --> 01:62:33,880
Really weird guy.

58
01:62:33,880 --> 01:62:34,880
What was this called?

59
01:62:34,880 --> 01:62:36,880
It's called Meet the Hitlers.

60
01:62:36,880 --> 01:62:39,880
Going back to There's Really Weird People in the World,

61
01:62:39,880 --> 01:62:42,880
it's a whole movie about people who refuse to change their name

62
01:62:42,880 --> 01:62:44,880
whenever their name is Hitler.

63
01:62:44,880 --> 01:62:45,880
It's like a new sitcom.

64
01:62:45,880 --> 01:62:48,880
Yes.

65
01:62:48,880 --> 01:62:50,880
So, yeah, it's all about these,

66
01:62:50,880 --> 01:62:54,880
I think they're all documented cases.

67
01:62:54,880 --> 01:62:56,880
All right, anyway.

68
01:62:56,880 --> 01:62:58,880
So, what does probability language do?

69
01:62:58,880 --> 01:63:00,880
It allows us to quantify uncertainty.

70
01:63:00,880 --> 01:63:02,880
So we live in an uncertain world.

71
01:63:02,880 --> 01:63:05,880
Very often, when someone asks you a question,

72
01:63:05,880 --> 01:63:07,880
it's tempting just to say, I don't know.

73
01:63:07,880 --> 01:63:09,880
But there is more that you could say.

74
01:63:09,880 --> 01:63:11,880
There is more nuance that you can add

75
01:63:11,880 --> 01:63:13,880
to your I don't know statements.

76
01:63:13,880 --> 01:63:15,880
And what probability language allows us to do

77
01:63:15,880 --> 01:63:17,880
is to actually put a number on it.

78
01:63:17,880 --> 01:63:21,880
To go and say, well, look, yes, I don't know.

79
01:63:21,880 --> 01:63:23,880
But what's your probability of having it?

80
01:63:23,880 --> 01:63:27,880
90%, or 10%, or 3%, or 1 in 100 billion.

81
01:63:27,880 --> 01:63:31,880
These are all possible probabilities.

82
01:63:31,880 --> 01:63:34,880
Now, there are two different interpretations of probability.

83
01:63:34,880 --> 01:63:37,880
There is the least controversial one,

84
01:63:37,880 --> 01:63:39,880
which is objective probability.

85
01:63:39,880 --> 01:63:41,880
Objective probability.

86
01:63:41,880 --> 01:63:44,880
All right, so even when we don't know what's going to happen,

87
01:63:44,880 --> 01:63:47,880
we can definitely talk about the relative frequencies

88
01:63:47,880 --> 01:63:50,880
of various observed events in the past.

89
01:63:50,880 --> 01:63:54,880
And if you know any Austrian economists here,

90
01:63:54,880 --> 01:63:56,880
or if you read them, they will say,

91
01:63:56,880 --> 01:63:58,880
we have no problem with objective probability.

92
01:63:58,880 --> 01:64:00,880
Objective probability is fine.

93
01:64:00,880 --> 01:64:03,880
So all you're doing is getting a tally

94
01:64:03,880 --> 01:64:05,880
of everything that's ever happened,

95
01:64:05,880 --> 01:64:08,880
and then going and saying, this is the percentage of times

96
01:64:08,880 --> 01:64:10,880
that all the doors I've ever seen are open,

97
01:64:10,880 --> 01:64:12,880
the percentage of times that they were closed.

98
01:64:12,880 --> 01:64:15,880
So the objective probability that a door is closed

99
01:64:15,880 --> 01:64:18,880
is the ratio of times that I saw doors closed

100
01:64:18,880 --> 01:64:20,880
to the number of doors that I've ever seen.

101
01:64:20,880 --> 01:64:22,880
OK, something like that.

102
01:64:22,880 --> 01:64:25,880
All right, so you can always talk about relative frequencies.

103
01:64:25,880 --> 01:64:29,880
But objective probability, though there are no

104
01:64:29,880 --> 01:64:31,880
philosophical complaints about it,

105
01:64:31,880 --> 01:64:34,880
is problematic in a lot of ways.

106
01:64:34,880 --> 01:64:37,880
The biggest problem is that according to objective probability,

107
01:64:37,880 --> 01:64:40,880
you cannot talk about the frequency of unique events.

108
01:64:40,880 --> 01:64:43,880
Because unique events has either already happened,

109
01:64:43,880 --> 01:64:45,880
in which case it's got a probability of 0 or 1.

110
01:64:45,880 --> 01:64:48,880
But then this doesn't tell us anything about anything

111
01:64:48,880 --> 01:64:50,880
that's ever going to happen, because those events

112
01:64:50,880 --> 01:64:52,880
are going to be unique events too.

113
01:64:52,880 --> 01:64:54,880
All right, so that is the problem.

114
01:64:54,880 --> 01:64:56,880
And here's the thing.

115
01:64:56,880 --> 01:64:58,880
If you really take the idea seriously,

116
01:64:58,880 --> 01:65:02,880
how many real world events are in fact unique?

117
01:65:02,880 --> 01:65:05,880
All events are unique.

118
01:65:05,880 --> 01:65:08,880
This event is unique, just like every other event.

119
01:65:08,880 --> 01:65:11,880
If you just think about, well,

120
01:65:11,880 --> 01:65:14,880
so what is the, like, all computer monitors

121
01:65:14,880 --> 01:65:17,880
in this room that have ever been in this room

122
01:65:17,880 --> 01:65:19,880
that have been tipped over by me

123
01:65:19,880 --> 01:65:22,880
on the first Monday of class in 2017,

124
01:65:22,880 --> 01:65:24,880
and now we're already down to unique events.

125
01:65:24,880 --> 01:65:27,880
It's the only one that fits that description.

126
01:65:27,880 --> 01:65:30,880
Or if you go and apply it even to games of chance,

127
01:65:30,880 --> 01:65:33,880
you say, what's the probability of royal flush?

128
01:65:33,880 --> 01:65:35,880
Well, what's the probability of getting a royal flush

129
01:65:35,880 --> 01:65:39,880
tonight in the MGM Grand Casino

130
01:65:39,880 --> 01:65:42,880
when a guy in a top hat just walked by,

131
01:65:42,880 --> 01:65:45,880
when Baz Luhrmann's musical is playing

132
01:65:45,880 --> 01:65:47,880
across the street in three hours,

133
01:65:47,880 --> 01:65:49,880
when just add on a number of conditions

134
01:65:49,880 --> 01:65:52,880
and pretty soon you've described a unique event.

135
01:65:52,880 --> 01:65:54,880
And then you have the objective probability

136
01:65:54,880 --> 01:65:56,880
of something that is never going,

137
01:65:56,880 --> 01:65:58,880
that you see that it will never happen again.

138
01:65:58,880 --> 01:66:00,880
So then what is the use?

139
01:66:00,880 --> 01:66:02,880
In any event you can describe that way.

140
01:66:02,880 --> 01:66:04,880
For anything that's happened, you can say,

141
01:66:04,880 --> 01:66:06,880
well, once you add some detail on.

142
01:66:06,880 --> 01:66:08,880
I remember when I was a kid, sometimes I would get

143
01:66:08,880 --> 01:66:10,880
this sense of metaphysical awe when I'd realize,

144
01:66:10,880 --> 01:66:13,880
you know, I'm probably the only person in the world today

145
01:66:13,880 --> 01:66:17,880
who read Encyclopedia Brown

146
01:66:17,880 --> 01:66:20,880
and then went and watched the Power Rangers

147
01:66:20,880 --> 01:66:24,880
and then went and got punched by his brother Gregory Randolph

148
01:66:24,880 --> 01:66:27,880
and then went and fed his cat, B-I-C,

149
01:66:27,880 --> 01:66:29,880
which stands for very important cat.

150
01:66:29,880 --> 01:66:31,880
So this is probably the only person,

151
01:66:31,880 --> 01:66:33,880
I'm the only person out of billions,

152
01:66:33,880 --> 01:66:35,880
and I'm the only one, I'm unique.

153
01:66:35,880 --> 01:66:37,880
And that was like, wow.

154
01:66:37,880 --> 01:66:40,880
You just need to add on a few layers of detail

155
01:66:40,880 --> 01:66:46,880
or if you have ever listened to your music mix on Shuffle,

156
01:66:46,880 --> 01:66:48,880
five songs, what are the odds that those five songs

157
01:66:48,880 --> 01:66:50,880
have ever been listened to by anybody else on earth

158
01:66:50,880 --> 01:66:53,880
in that exact order?

159
01:66:53,880 --> 01:66:55,880
Probably very low, unless you're like,

160
01:66:55,880 --> 01:67:00,880
you've only won an album on your player.

161
01:67:00,880 --> 01:67:02,880
Okay.

162
01:67:02,880 --> 01:67:05,880
Alright, so anyway, if you take this idea seriously,

163
01:67:05,880 --> 01:67:07,880
you realize every event is strictly speaking unique,

164
01:67:07,880 --> 01:67:09,880
so you can never apply probability to the real world.

165
01:67:09,880 --> 01:67:11,880
When I made this point in a debate with Walter Block,

166
01:67:11,880 --> 01:67:14,880
he just said, great, even better.

167
01:67:14,880 --> 01:67:16,880
I never liked probability anyway.

168
01:67:16,880 --> 01:67:19,880
So you can't even apply it to poker anymore.

169
01:67:19,880 --> 01:67:20,880
You can't apply it to the lottery.

170
01:67:20,880 --> 01:67:22,880
You can't apply it to dice.

171
01:67:22,880 --> 01:67:24,880
So it just doesn't apply.

172
01:67:24,880 --> 01:67:27,880
Of course, most people find this idea odd.

173
01:67:27,880 --> 01:67:29,880
You can never talk about probabilities of anything.

174
01:67:29,880 --> 01:67:35,880
You can't say, look, well, we've never played the lottery

175
01:67:35,880 --> 01:67:39,880
on February 1st, 2017, so we can't actually talk

176
01:67:39,880 --> 01:67:41,880
about the probability of winning.

177
01:67:41,880 --> 01:67:42,880
It's just not allowed.

178
01:67:42,880 --> 01:67:43,880
There's no way we can say it.

179
01:67:43,880 --> 01:67:45,880
Any claim about it is meaningless.

180
01:67:45,880 --> 01:67:49,880
So that seems like a strange conclusion,

181
01:67:49,880 --> 01:67:52,880
although you can always bite these bullets if you want to.

182
01:67:52,880 --> 01:67:55,880
Alright, so this leads us to a much more useful

183
01:67:55,880 --> 01:67:58,880
but of course more controversial concept of probability,

184
01:67:58,880 --> 01:68:00,880
which is subject to probability.

185
01:68:00,880 --> 01:68:03,880
Subject to probability is simply the degree of belief

186
01:68:03,880 --> 01:68:06,880
a person assigns to a proposition, and that is all.

187
01:68:06,880 --> 01:68:08,880
Alright, probability is some simple axioms.

188
01:68:08,880 --> 01:68:13,880
Beliefs range from impossible to certain,

189
01:68:13,880 --> 01:68:15,880
possible corresponding to probability of zero,

190
01:68:15,880 --> 01:68:16,880
certain to probability of one.

191
01:68:16,880 --> 01:68:19,880
Are there any math majors in this room?

192
01:68:19,880 --> 01:68:21,880
Okay, so if you are a math major,

193
01:68:21,880 --> 01:68:22,880
you know that technically speaking,

194
01:68:22,880 --> 01:68:24,880
an event can have a probability of one

195
01:68:24,880 --> 01:68:26,880
but not be truly certain, and you can have a probability

196
01:68:26,880 --> 01:68:28,880
of zero but not be impossible.

197
01:68:28,880 --> 01:68:30,880
There could be an infinitesimal probability,

198
01:68:30,880 --> 01:68:32,880
but we're not going to worry about that.

199
01:68:32,880 --> 01:68:36,880
Alright, okay, and since something always happens,

200
01:68:36,880 --> 01:68:38,880
right, something always happens,

201
01:68:38,880 --> 01:68:42,880
then the sum of all possible events must equal one,

202
01:68:42,880 --> 01:68:44,880
because it is certain that something will happen.

203
01:68:44,880 --> 01:68:48,880
Okay, so the idea of subjective probability is for any event.

204
01:68:48,880 --> 01:68:51,880
So how confident are you on the spectrum

205
01:68:51,880 --> 01:68:54,880
from it being certain with probability of zero

206
01:68:54,880 --> 01:68:56,880
to be certain or possible with probability of zero

207
01:68:56,880 --> 01:68:58,880
to certain with probability of one?

208
01:68:58,880 --> 01:69:01,880
You could be 0.9, so I assign probability of 0.9 to it,

209
01:69:01,880 --> 01:69:03,880
probability of 0.3, 0.78,

210
01:69:03,880 --> 01:69:05,880
there could be any probabilities that you can assign

211
01:69:05,880 --> 01:69:07,880
to any event, right, all, and I'll have,

212
01:69:07,880 --> 01:69:10,880
where do these probabilities come from?

213
01:69:10,880 --> 01:69:11,880
Just from inside your head.

214
01:69:11,880 --> 01:69:14,880
How confident are you of this?

215
01:69:14,880 --> 01:69:16,880
Now of course, the fact that someone states

216
01:69:16,880 --> 01:69:19,880
high or low confidence doesn't mean they really believe it.

217
01:69:19,880 --> 01:69:22,880
One of the best ways of eliciting probabilities

218
01:69:22,880 --> 01:69:26,880
that people genuinely, sincerely, honestly, earnestly believe

219
01:69:26,880 --> 01:69:29,880
is to offer to bet them based upon their announced probabilities.

220
01:69:29,880 --> 01:69:31,880
And if you know anything about me at all,

221
01:69:31,880 --> 01:69:32,880
this is one of my favorite things to do,

222
01:69:32,880 --> 01:69:34,880
is when people say things that I don't believe

223
01:69:34,880 --> 01:69:37,880
even they believe, I challenge them to bet me on it.

224
01:69:37,880 --> 01:69:40,880
And usually I find that when you do this,

225
01:69:40,880 --> 01:69:42,880
people run for the hills like frightened rabbits

226
01:69:42,880 --> 01:69:44,880
because they are just shooting their mouth off

227
01:69:44,880 --> 01:69:48,880
and they are using language that appears

228
01:69:48,880 --> 01:69:50,880
to be very quantitatively precise,

229
01:69:50,880 --> 01:69:54,880
but actually is just hot air.

230
01:69:54,880 --> 01:69:57,880
There's also a lot of great empirical research

231
01:69:57,880 --> 01:69:59,880
on subjective probabilities.

232
01:69:59,880 --> 01:70:01,880
So beginning in, I believe, the 60s,

233
01:70:01,880 --> 01:70:03,880
some psychologists went and tried to find out

234
01:70:03,880 --> 01:70:06,880
for the average person, what does,

235
01:70:06,880 --> 01:70:11,880
what does the English phrase probably mean in numbers?

236
01:70:11,880 --> 01:70:14,880
So when someone says X will probably happen,

237
01:70:14,880 --> 01:70:17,880
what probability between zero and one does that correspond to?

238
01:70:17,880 --> 01:70:20,880
And what they found was, frighteningly,

239
01:70:20,880 --> 01:70:25,880
the range is somewhere between 20 and 90% for people.

240
01:70:25,880 --> 01:70:28,880
That's what they think of as probably.

241
01:70:28,880 --> 01:70:31,880
And then there's many other things.

242
01:70:31,880 --> 01:70:33,880
How about almost certain?

243
01:70:33,880 --> 01:70:36,880
Almost certain varies from, like,

244
01:70:36,880 --> 01:70:39,880
65% up to 100%.

245
01:70:39,880 --> 01:70:42,880
And again, this is not just like the most and least extreme.

246
01:70:42,880 --> 01:70:44,880
This is, again, this is maybe like

247
01:70:44,880 --> 01:70:47,880
the 25th and 75th percentile of answers.

248
01:70:47,880 --> 01:70:50,880
So, you know, like, to me,

249
01:70:50,880 --> 01:70:52,880
at least I always thought, look,

250
01:70:52,880 --> 01:70:55,880
probably clearly means 0.5, you know, greater than 0.5.

251
01:70:55,880 --> 01:70:57,880
Like, how could it mean anything else?

252
01:70:57,880 --> 01:70:58,880
I don't know.

253
01:70:58,880 --> 01:71:02,880
Actually, English is way vaguer than I realized, most people.

254
01:71:02,880 --> 01:71:05,880
Now, this was actually done in the context of

255
01:71:05,880 --> 01:71:07,880
the United States Intelligence Services,

256
01:71:07,880 --> 01:71:09,880
and after it was pointed out, a suggestion was made

257
01:71:09,880 --> 01:71:12,880
that they replace all this verbiage with numbers,

258
01:71:12,880 --> 01:71:14,880
and a decision was made to not do that.

259
01:71:14,880 --> 01:71:19,880
To keep using vague adjectives rather than precise numbers,

260
01:71:19,880 --> 01:71:23,880
which does reduce conflict among the people making,

261
01:71:23,880 --> 01:71:25,880
writing the reports,

262
01:71:25,880 --> 01:71:28,880
although in terms of actually conveying useful information,

263
01:71:28,880 --> 01:71:30,880
on the other hand, it's terrible, but...

264
01:71:30,880 --> 01:71:32,880
Is it possible people writing the reports

265
01:71:32,880 --> 01:71:34,880
care more about getting along with each other

266
01:71:34,880 --> 01:71:36,880
than actually conveying accurate information?

267
01:71:36,880 --> 01:71:40,880
There is overwhelming evidence that this is so, I would say.

268
01:71:40,880 --> 01:71:42,880
All right.

269
01:71:42,880 --> 01:71:44,880
So, in any case...

270
01:71:44,880 --> 01:71:47,880
So, you know, so...

271
01:71:47,880 --> 01:71:49,880
The idea of subjective probability

272
01:71:49,880 --> 01:71:52,880
is to assign a number between 0 and 1 corresponding to it.

273
01:71:52,880 --> 01:71:54,880
We might hope that this is just giving

274
01:71:54,880 --> 01:71:57,880
a little bit more structure to normal English adjectives,

275
01:71:57,880 --> 01:72:00,880
although when we actually talk to normal English speakers,

276
01:72:00,880 --> 01:72:02,880
it appears it's going way beyond.

277
01:72:02,880 --> 01:72:05,880
It's taking words that we use almost as gibberish

278
01:72:05,880 --> 01:72:08,880
and replacing them with a number that finally means something.

279
01:72:08,880 --> 01:72:11,880
Right? Of course, even that has problems,

280
01:72:11,880 --> 01:72:15,880
but at least my.2 is not different from your.2.

281
01:72:15,880 --> 01:72:17,880
All right.

282
01:72:17,880 --> 01:72:19,880
Now, the main objection to subjective probability

283
01:72:19,880 --> 01:72:21,880
is just that it's unrealistic,

284
01:72:21,880 --> 01:72:23,880
because normal human beings rarely assign

